A||N||8||Wrench||Red Hot Manifold||||9||
Z||000000||Wrench||01-12-2000||08:04 PM||||Here we go. 86 GT V6, no mods, 135,000 miles. My rear exhaust manifold, the one by the firewall, is running red hot. the whole manifold literall glows red hot! Front exhaust manifold is cracked but does not glow. Only disabled piece is the EGR due to a broken tube. That was over two years ago however. Plugged cat? Any help appreciated as this is my daily driver to and from work.||||reg||9
Z||000001||DRH||01-12-2000||08:20 PM||||A leaking EGR tube will make the manifold glow. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wrench||01-12-2000||08:33 PM||||When my tube cracked we trashed the tube and blanked off both flanges with flat piecs of 1/4" plate. This was two years ago and no manifold glow at that time. By the way my plugs look very white. No codes stored either.||||reg||9
Z||000003||dogtired||01-12-2000||08:35 PM||||My timing was off just a little, didn't quite have it averaged between the #1 and #4 which caused mine to glow.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>Once I tweeked the distributor just a tad it cleared up? <BR>It worked for me???   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   GOOD LUCK!||||reg||6
Z||000004||Ed||01-12-2000||09:27 PM||||My V-6 manifolds got red hot when my catalytic converter died. But the car ran really badly and it was obvious that something was radically wrong.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||01-12-2000||10:52 PM||||My first three ideas were an inoperative EGR system, plugged cat, and timing off. What may be happening is a combination of several of these problems. First, then entire purpose for the EGR system is to <B>lower</B> combustion temperatures. Very easy to burn a whole through a piston(white plugs also indicate very high combustion temps). With the timing off you can also get very high combustion temps resulting in the glowing manifold. Third, a plugged cat will increase backpressure, resulting in the glowing manifold.<BR>I think it is a combination of no EGR, and at least one other problem. Either a cat going south, or timing off by a hair.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mshill||01-13-2000||03:46 AM||||Seems to reason that if the timing was off then both manifolds would be glowing.  Since the one that is not glowing has a crack, it is likely to have less backpressure.  I vote for plugged cat.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||01-14-2000||09:38 PM||||I vote plugged catalyst to.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Wrench||01-15-2000||05:44 PM||||Thanks for the replys. It was the Cat. I also replaced the plug wires(Taylor Spiro Pro, #74224), plugs, Dist Cap and rotor. Timing was 8 degrees and I reset it to 10. Oh I also put an MSD 8226 coil. Guess it was time to spend a few bucks after 14 years of good service. By the way my eng temp dropped 20 degrees after the cat was removed and I have no pinging any more. New EGR tube enroute from GM dealer($76.00. This will put her all back as designed!!||||reg||14
